{
	title: 'Web disclosure controls for short, glimpse and long',
	spec: `The web reaches short, glimpse and long through controls placed by what they change; the terminal keeps the short, glimpse, long cycle on Ctrl-O and /toggle, because it cannot target parts of a card (a recorded UI exception: the input methods differ).

Web header: a small disclosure triangle before the time, pointing right when the card is short and down when it is open, turned 90 degrees with a CSS transform transition. A tap on the header or the triangle switches short and open; open returns to the glimpse or long the card had before. A tap on the body still collapses the card, until the user has tested selection and scrolling on a phone.

Web glimpse of a tool card: the command on one line, cut with an ellipsis, then the glimpse rows of output, then 'Show all'. A command toggle appears only when the command was really cut (more than one line, or measured overflow in the DOM); it shows the whole command and back. Only the 'Show all' / 'Show less' line switches glimpse and long.

Terminal glimpse of a Bash card: the command's first line, cut with '…', then the glimpse rows of output, the same height while running and after (a whole multi-line command made a glimpse about 20 rows tall).`,
	status: 'planned',
	needs: ['hyt'],
	foldInto: ['hyt']
}
